rem =========================================================================
rem BENCHMARK CONFIGURATION
rem =========================================================================
rem This file configures the parameters for running the HBPL engine tests.
rem It supports two execution modes: Single Benchmark and Automated Sequence.
rem
rem 1. SINGLE BENCHMARK MODE (Default)
rem    - Triggered when all 'Automate...' variables below are commented out (rem).
rem    - The script executes a single test instance using the exact values
rem      defined in the static variables: Equations, Rules, HypoRules, and Assumptions.
rem    - Example: Running with exactly 300 HypoRules, 1 Equation, and 0 Rules.
rem
rem 2. AUTOMATED SEQUENCE / SCALED MULTI-STEP MODE
rem    - Triggered by uncommenting (removing 'rem') any 'Automate...' variable.
rem    - Format: set Automate[Param]=Min_Value Max_Value Number_of_Steps
rem    - The script will dynamically calculate equidistant steps from the minimum
rem      to the maximum value and execute them in a nested loop hierarchy.
rem    - Example: 'set AutomateHypoRules=100 350 9' will override 'HypoRules'
rem      and run 9 benchmark runs at steps: 100, 131, 162, 193, 225, 256, 287, 318, 350.
rem
rem NOTES:
rem    - Benchmark is the name of the benchmark, used to insert the benchmark name in
rem      the term data as generated by run_all_benchmarks.bat.
rem    - Runs is the number of runs to be collected (one more is initially executed to
rem      discard setup factors). 
rem    - HypoRules and AutomateHypoRules are only applicable to stocks-r.
rem    - Equations is the number of equations for the block young-middle (at least 1).
rem    - Rules is the number of rules without assumptions in the rule base.
rem    - HypoRules is the number of rules with body assumptions in the rule base.
rem    - Assumptions is the number of assumptions in the goal to be measured.
rem    - The current active values of each parameter are preserved in their
rem      respective environment variables for compatibility with external scripts.
rem =========================================================================

rem IMPORTANT:
rem     - Modify the next path to match your SWI-Prolog installation
rem       but note that version 7.6.4 is required
rem     - The following values for the environment variables define a single run for
rem       the stocks example in Figure 2 of the paper (maybe with different degrees),
rem       with a benchmark name 'stocks'. Values for Equations, Rules, HypoRules and
rem       Assumptions can be set for trying specific points of the experiments in the
rem       paper. To automate scalings, use the Automate... variables as noted above.
set SWIPL="C:\Program Files\SWI-Prolog 7.6.4\bin\swipl.exe" -L2g -G1g
set Benchmark=stocks-n
set Runs=1
set Equations=1
set Rules=0
set HypoRules=0
set Assumptions=0
rem set AutomateEquations=10 80 9
rem set AutomateAssumptions=1000 4200 9
rem set AutomateRules=1000 4200 9
rem set AutomateHypoRules=100 350 9
exit /b